'''''Bochasanwasi Akshar Purushottam Swaminarayan Sanstha''''', short '''BAPS''', is a [[Hindu denomination]] within the [[Swaminarayan Sampradaya]].{{sfn|Mamtora|2021}}{{sfn|A.Patel|2018}}{{sfn|Gadhia|2016|p=157}} It was formed in 1905 by Shastri Yagnapurushdas ([[Shastriji Maharaj]]) following his conviction that the yogi [[Swaminarayan]] remained present on earth through a lineage of gurus starting with [[Gunatitanand Swami]].{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=55}}{{sfn|Rinehart|2004|p=215}}{{sfn|James|2017}} As of August 2016, Mahant Swami Maharaj is the 6th guru and president of BAPS.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Kulkarni |first=Deepali |date=December 2018 |title=Digital Mūrtis, Virtual Darśan and a Hindu Religioscape |url=https://journals.co.za/content/journal/10520/EJC-1273db35e9 |journal=Nidān: International Journal for Indian Studies |volume=3 |issue=2 |pages=40–54}}</ref>

The philosophy of BAPS is centred on the doctrine of [[Akshar-Purushottam Upasana]], in which followers worship [[Swaminarayan]] as God, or Purushottam, and his choicest devotee, [[Gunatitanand Swami]], as Akshar.{{sfn|Williams|1984|p=73}} As of 2024, BAPS has 44 [[shikharbaddha mandir]]s and more than 1,300 [[Hindu temple|mandirs]] worldwide that facilitate practice of this doctrine by allowing followers to offer devotion to the [[murti]]s of Swaminarayan, Gunatitanand Swami, and their successors.<ref>{{cite web |title=BAPS Global Network |url=https://www.baps.org/Global-Network.aspx}}</ref> BAPS mandirs also feature activities to foster culture and youth development. Many devotees view the mandir as a place for transmission of Hindu values and their incorporation into daily routines, family life, and careers.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=418–422}}<ref group=web name="India Herald Spiritual Quotient"/>

==History==

=== Formation and early years (1900-1950) ===
[[File:Akshar Purshottam - BAPS Mandir Bochasan.jpg|thumb|Murtis of Akshar Purushottam, Bochasan]]

==== The Gunatit Guru ====
The basis for the formation of BAPS was Shastriji Maharaj's conviction that [[Swaminarayan]] remained present on earth through a lineage of ''Gunatit Gurus'' (perfect devotee), starting with [[Gunatitanand Swami]], one of Swaminarayan's most prominent disciples,{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=55}}{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=317}}{{sfn|Kim|2005}}{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=363}}{{sfn|Rinehart|2004|p=215}}{{sfn|Paranjape|2005|pp=117, 129}}{{refn|group=note|In many of his discourses in the Vachanamrut (Gadhada I-71,{{sfn|Swaminarayan|2006|pp=147–148}} Gadhada III-26{{sfn|Swaminarayan|2006|pp=630–631}} and Vadtal 5{{sfn|Swaminarayan|2006|p=534}}) [[Swaminarayan]] explains that there forever exists a Gunatit Guru{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=363}} (perfect devotee) through whom Swaminarayan manifests on earth{{sfn|Williams|1984|p=92}} for the ultimate redemption of jivas.{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=178}}}} and that Swaminarayan and his choicest devotee, Gunatitanand Swami, were ontologically, Purushottam and Akshar, respectively.{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=186}}{{refn|group=note|According to the BAPS, numerous historical accounts{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=89}}<ref name="Acharya 1911 13">{{cite book|title=Shri Kirtan Kaustubhmala (1st ed.)|last=Acharya|first=Viharilalji Maharaj|publisher=Published by Kothari Hathibhai Nanjibhai by the order of Acharya Shripatiprasadji Maharaj and Kothari Gordhanbhai|year=1911|page=13}}</ref> and texts<ref name="Raghuvirji Maharaj 1966">{{cite book|title=Shri Harililakalpataru (1st ed.)|last=Raghuvirji Maharaj|first=Acharya|publisher=Sheth Maneklal Chunilal Nanavati Publishers|year=1966 |translator=Sadhu Shwetvaikunthdas |location=Mumbai}}</ref> written during Swaminarayan and Gunatitanand Swami's time period identify Gunatitanand Swami as [[Gunatitanand Swami#Gunatitanand Swami as Akshar|the embodiment of Akshar]].}} According to the BAPS-tradition, Shastriji Maharaj had understood this from his guru, [[Bhagatji Maharaj]], who had Gunatitanand Swami as his guru.{{sfn|Paranjape|2005|p=117}}{{refn|group=note|Bhagatji Maharaj over time came to believe that the doctrine of Akshar-Purushottam Upasana was the true doctrine propagated by Swaminarayan.<ref group=web>{{Cite web | url=http://www.swaminarayan.org/bhagatjimaharaj/life/10.htm |title = Bhagatji Maharaj's Life-Realisation}}</ref> Bhagatji Maharaj was Shastriji Maharaj's guru, and over time, Shastriji Maharaj also became a strong proponent of the [[Akshar-Purushottam Darshan|Akshar-Purushottam Upasana]].{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=55}} After Bhagatji Maharaj died on 7 November 1897,<ref>{{cite book|last=Ishwarcharandas|first=Sadhu|title=Pragji Bhakta – A short biography of Brahmaswarup Bhagatji Maharaj|year=1978|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|location=Ahmedabad|page=65}}</ref> Shastriji Maharaj became the primary proponent of the doctrine of Akshar-Purushottam Upasana.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=55}}}}

Followers of BAPS believe that the Ekantik dharma that Swaminarayan desired to establish is embodied and propagated by the ''Ekantik Satpurush'' ({{lit|a most sublime saint}}<ref group=web>BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha, [https://www.swaminarayan.org/shastrijimaharaj/life/14.htm Bhagatji: The Greatest Ekantik Saint]</ref>{{refn|group=note|Ekantik: "complete", "absolute";<ref group=web>Sanskrit Dictionary for Spoken Sanskrit, [https://www.learnsanskrit.cc/index.php?mode=3&direct=au&script=hk&tran_input=ekantik ekantik]</ref> Satpurush: "good or wise man".<ref group=web>Sanskrit Dictionary for Spoken Sanskrit, [https://www.learnsanskrit.cc/index.php?mode=3&direct=au&script=hk&tran_input=satpurush satpurush]</ref>}}), the Gunatit Guru.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=327, 358}} According to Shastriji Maharaj, Swaminarayan had "expressly designated" the Gunatit Guru to spiritually guide the satsang (spiritual fellowship) while instructing his nephews to help manage the administration of the fellowship within their respective dioceses.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=317}}<ref name="Williams2008">{{cite book|last=Williams|first=Raymond Brady|title=Encyclopedia of new religious movements Swaminarayan Hinduism Founder: Sahajanand Swami Country of origin: India|year=2008|publisher=Routledge|location=London|isbn=978-0-415-45383-7|edition=1. publ.}}</ref>{{rp|610}} As Kim notes, "For BAPS devotees, the dual murtis in the original Swaminarayan temples{{refn|group=note|name=murtis}} imply that Swaminarayan did install a murti of himself alongside the murti of his ideal bhakta or Guru".{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=364}}

Shastriji Maharaj sought to publicly reveal his ideas,{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=186}} and to worship Gunatitanand as the abode of Purushottam, c.q. Swaminarayan.{{sfn|James|2017}} However, his views were rejected by the sadhus of the Vadtal and Ahmedabad dioceses.{{sfn|Williams|1984|p=55}}{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=55}}{{sfn|Williams|2018|p=61}}{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=364–365}} For the sadhus of the Vadtal diocese, the idea that Swaminarayan had appointed Gunatitanand as his spiritual successor, instead of the two acharyas, was a heretical teaching, and they "refused to worship what they considered to be a human being."{{sfn|Williams|2018|p=93}}<!-- **START OF NOTE** -->{{refn|group=note|According to the BAPS, the fundamental beliefs of the BAPS date back to the time of Swaminarayan.<ref>Amrutvijaydas, Sadhu (2006). Shastriji Maharaj Life and Work. Amdavad: Swaminarayan Aksharpith.{{ISBN|81-7526-305-9}}.</ref> One revelation of Gunatitanand Swami as Akshar occurred in 1810 at the grand yagna of Dabhan, during which Swaminarayan initiated Gunatitanand Swami as a swami. On this occasion, Swaminarayan publicly confirmed that Gunatitanand Swami was the incarnation of Akshar, declaring, "Today, I am extremely happy to initiate Mulji Sharma. He is my divine abode – Akshardham, which is infinite and endless." The first Acharya of the Vadtal diocese, Raghuvirji Maharaj, recorded this declaration in his composition, the Harililakalpataru (7.17.49–50).<ref>Acharya, Raghuvirji Maharaj; Sadhu Shwetvaikunthdas (Translator) (1966). Shri Harililakalpataru (1st ed.). Mumbai: Sheth Maneklal Chunilal Nanavati Publishers.</ref> The ''Swaminarayan-Bhashyam'' is a published commentary written by [[Bhadreshdas Swami]] in 2007 that explicates the roots of Akshar-Purushottam Upasana in the [[Upanishads]], the [[Bhagavad Gita]], and the [[Brahma Sutras]].<ref name=":22">{{Cite book|last1=Amrutvijaydas|first1=Sadhu|title=Swaminarayan and British Contacts in Gujarat in the 1820s|last2=Williams|first2=Raymond Brady|last3=Paramtattvadas|first3=Sadhu|date=2016-04-01|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199086573|language=en-US|doi=10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199463749.003.0005}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Bhadreshdas|first=Sadhu|date=April–June 2014|title=Guru's grace empowers philosophical treatise|url=https://www.hinduismtoday.com/modules/smartsection/item.php?itemid=5495|journal=Hinduism Today}}</ref>  This is further corroborated in a classical Sanskrit treatise, also authored by Bhadreshdas Swami, called ''Swaminarayan-Siddhanta-Sudha''.}}<!-- **END OF NOTE** --> Shastriji Maharaj left Vadtal with five swamis and the support of about 150 devotees.{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=363–365}}{{sfn|Williams|2001|pp=54–56}}<ref name="100y">{{cite book |first=Sadhu |last=Amrutvijaydas |title=100 Years of BAPS |year=2007 |publisher=Aksharpith|location=Amdavad|isbn=978-81-7526-377-2 |url=http://www.swaminarayan.org/publications/books/100yearsofbaps.htm}}</ref>{{rp|13}}

==== Mandirs to facilitate doctrinal practice ====
[[File:Shastriji_Maharaj_Sarangpur.jpg|alt=|thumb|[[Shastriji Maharaj]]|266x266px]]

Paralleling Sahajand Swami's building of temples to propagate his teachings,{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=364}}<!-- **START OF NOTE** -->{{refn|group=note|name=murtis|From 1822 to 1828, Swaminarayan constructed a total of six shikharbaddha mandirs in Gujarat to propagate his teachings; in each he installed the murtis of a principal deity coupled with their ideal devotee in the central shrine: [[Nar Narayan Dev|Nar-Narayan-Dev]] in [[Kalupur Swaminarayan Mandir|Ahmedabad]] (1822) and [[Shri Swaminarayan Mandir, Bhuj|Bhuj]] (1823), Lakshmi-Narayan-Dev in [[Shri Swaminarayan Mandir, Vadtal|Vadtal]] (1824), [[Madan Mohan Dev|Madan-Mohan-Dev]] in [[Shri Swaminarayan Mandir, Dholera|Dholera]] (1826), Radha-Raman-Dev in [[Shri Swaminarayan Mandir, Junagadh|Junagadh]] (1828), and Gopi-Nathji in [[Shri Swaminarayan Mandir, Gadhada|Gadhada]] (1828).{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=364–365}}}}<!-- **END OF NOTE** --> Shastriji Maharaj then set out to build his own ''mandir'' to "house the devotional representations of [[Bhagavan|Bhagwan]] and [[Guru]]" and propagate his understanding of Swaminarayan's teachings.{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=363}} On 5 June 1907, Shastriji Maharaj consecrated the murtis of [[Swaminarayan]] and [[Gunatitanand Swami]] in the central shrine of the shikharbaddha mandir he was constructing in the village of Bochasan in the Kheda District of Gujarat.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=91}} This event was later seen to mark the formal establishment of the Bochasanwasi Akshar Purushottam Swaminarayan Sanstha,{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=364}} which was later abbreviated as BAPS. The Gujarati word Bochasanwasi implies hailing from Bochasan, since the organisation's first mandir was built in this village.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}

Shastriji Maharaj spent the majority of 1908–15 discoursing throughout Gujarat, while continuing construction work of mandirs in Bochasan and Sarangpur, gaining a group of devotees, admirers, and supporters.{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=365}} Over the next four decades, Shastriji Maharaj completed four more shikharbaddha mandirs in Gujarat (Sarangpur – 1916, Gondal – 1934, Atladra – 1945, and Gadhada – 1951).{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=365}}

==== Successors ====
On 12 August 1910 Shastriji Maharaj met his eventual successor, [[Sadhu Gnãnjivandas|Yogiji Maharaj]], at the house of Jadavji in Bochasan.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|16}} Yogiji Maharaj was a resident swami at Junagadh Mandir (Saurashtra),{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=183}} where Gunatitanand Swami had served as mahant.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|17}} Yogiji Maharaj regarded Gunatitanand Swami as Akshar and also served the murti of Harikrishna Maharaj which had previously been worshiped by Gunatitanand Swami.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|17}} As he already believed in the doctrine being preached by Shastriji Maharaj, Yogiji Maharaj left Junagadh on 9 July 1911 with six swamis to join Shastriji Maharaj's mission.{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=186}}

On 7 November 1939, 17-year-old Shantilal Patel (who would become [[Pramukh Swami Maharaj]]) left his home<ref>{{cite book|last=Shelat|first=Kirit|title=Yug Purush Pujya Pramukh Swami Maharaj – a life dedicated to others|year=2005|publisher=Shri Bhagwati Trust|location=Ahmedabad|page=7}}</ref> and was initiated by Shastriji Maharaj into the parshad order, as Shanti Bhagat, on 22 November 1939,<ref name="Shelat 2005 8">{{cite book|last=Shelat|first=Kirit|title=Yug Purush Pujya Pramukh Swami Maharaj – a life dedicated to others|year=2005|publisher=Shri Bhagwati Trust|location=Ahmedabad|page=8}}</ref> and into the swami order, as Narayanswarupdas Swami, on 10 January 1940.<ref name="Shelat 2005 8"/> Initially, he studied Sanskrit and Hindu scriptures<ref name="Shelat 2005 8"/> and served as Shastriji Maharaj's personal secretary. In 1946, he was appointed administrative head (''Kothari'') of the Sarangpur mandir.<ref name="Shelat 2005 8"/>

In the early part of 1950, Shastriji Maharaj wrote several letters to 28-year-old Shastri Narayanswarupdas expressing a wish to appoint him as the administrative president of the organisation. Initially, Shastri Narayanswarupdas was reluctant to accept the position, citing his young age and lack of experience and suggesting that an elderly, experienced swami should take the responsibility.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Vivekjivandas|first=Sadhu|title=Pramukh Varni Din|journal=Swaminarayan Bliss|date=June 2000}}</ref> However, Shastriji Maharaj insisted over several months, until, seeing the wish and insistence of his guru, Shastri Narayanswarupdas accepted the responsibility.<ref name="Shelat 2005 8"/> On 21 May 1950 at Ambli-Vali Pol in Amdavad, Shastriji Maharaj appointed Shastri Narayanswarupdas as the administrative president (Pramukh) of BAPS.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|11}} He instructed Shastri Narayanswarupdas, who now began to be referred to as Pramukh Swami, to ennoble Satsang under the guidance of Yogiji Maharaj.<ref>{{cite web |title=Pramukh Varni Din |url=http://www.swaminarayan.org/festivals/pramukhvarni/ |work=Swaminarayan|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith |access-date=4 August 2013}}</ref>

In the last few years of his life, Shastriji Maharaj took steps to preserve the growth and future of BAPS by registering BAPS as a charitable trust in 1947 under India's new legal code.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|33}}

===Development and organisational formation (1950–1971)===

After the death of [[Shastriji Maharaj]] on 10 May 1951,<ref>{{cite book|last=Dave|first=Kishore M.|title=Shastriji Maharaj – A brief biography of Brahmaswarup Shastriji Maharaj|year=2009|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|location=Ahmedabad|isbn=978-81-7526-129-7|page=111}}</ref> [[Yogiji Maharaj]] became the spiritual leader, or guru, of the organisation while [[Pramukh Swami Maharaj|Pramukh Swami]] continued to oversee administrative matters as president of the organisation.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=60}} Yogiji Maharaj carried Shastriji Maharaj's mission of fostering the Akshar-Purushottam Upasana doctrine by building temples, touring villages, preaching overseas and initiating weekly local religious assemblies for children, youths and elders. In his 20 years as guru, from 1951 to 1971, he visited over 4,000 cities, towns and villages, consecrated over 60 mandirs and wrote over 5,45,000 letters to devotees.<ref name="100y" />{{rp|9}}

==== Youth movement ====
This period of BAPS history saw an important expansion in youth activities. [[Yogiji Maharaj]] believed that in a time of profound and rapid social ferment, there was an imminent need to save the young from 'degeneration of moral, cultural and religious values'.<ref name="Brear 1996">{{cite book|title=A sacred thread: modern transmission of Hindu traditions in India and abroad|year=1996|publisher=Columbia University Press|location=New York|isbn=0-231-10779-X|first=Douglas|last=Brear|edition=Columbia University Press|contribution=Transmission of sacred scripture in the British East Midlands: the Vachanamritam |editor=Raymond Brady Williams}}</ref>{{rp|219}} To fill a void in spiritual activities for youths, Yogiji Maharaj started a regular Sunday gathering (Yuvak Mandal) of young men in Bombay<ref name="Brear 1996"/>{{rp|217}} in 1952.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|167}} Brear notes, "His flair, dynamism and concern led within ten years to the establishment of many yuvak mandals of dedicated young men in Gujarat and East Africa".<ref name="Brear 1996"/>{{rp|217}}
In addition to providing religious and spiritual guidance, Yogiji Maharaj encouraged youths to work hard and excel in their studies. Towards realizing such ideals, he would often remind them to stay away from worldly temptations.<ref>{{cite web |title=Yogiji Maharaj – Sermons of Yogiji Maharaj |url=http://www.swaminarayan.org/yogijimaharaj/life/32.htm |work=Swaminarayan|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|access-date=4 August 2013}}</ref> A number of youths decided to take monastic vows.{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=187}} On 11 May 1961 during the Gadhada Kalash Mahotsav, he initiated 51 college-educated youths into the monastic order as swamis.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|168}} [[Mahant Swami Maharaj]], initiated as Keshavjivandas Swami, was one of the initiates.

==== East Africa ====
Satsang in Africa had started during Shastriji Maharaj's lifetime, as many devotees had migrated to Africa for economic reasons. One of Shastriji Maharaj's senior swamis, Nirgundas Swami, engaged in lengthy correspondence with these devotees, answering their questions and inspiring them to start satsang assemblies in Africa. Eventually, in 1928, Harman Patel took the murtis of Akshar-Purushottam Maharaj to East Africa and started a small centre.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|20}} Soon, the East Africa Satsang Mandal was established under the leadership of Harman Patel and Magan Patel.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|20}}

In 1955, Yogiji Maharaj embarked on his first foreign tour to East Africa.<ref name="Brear 1996"/>{{rp|217}}<ref name=":7" />{{rp|Chp 5 – Pg 2}} The prime reason for the visit was to consecrate Africa's first Akshar-Purushottam temple in Mombasa. The temple was inaugurated on 25 April 1955.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|168}}<ref name="Younger 2010 266">{{cite book|last=Younger|first=Paul|title=New homelands: Hindu communities in Mauritius, Guyana, Trinidad, South Africa, Fiji, and East Africa|year=2010|publisher=Oxford University Press|location=Oxford|isbn=978-0-19-539164-0|page=266|edition=[Online-Ausg.]}}</ref> He also travelled to [[Nairobi]], [[Nakuru]], [[Kisumu]], [[Tororo]], [[Jinja, Uganda|Jinja]], [[Kampala]], [[Mwanza]] and [[Dar es salaam]].<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|168}} His travels inspired the local devotees to begin temple construction projects. Due to the visit, in a span of five years, the devotees in Uganda completed the construction of temples in Tororo, Jinja and Kampala and asked Yogiji Maharaj to revisit Uganda to install the murtis of Akshar-Purushottam Maharaj. The rapid temple constructions in Africa were helped by the presence of early immigrants, mainly Leva Patels, who came to work as masons, and were particularly skilled in temple building.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Salvadori|first=Cynthia|title=Revivals, reformations & revisions of Hinduism; the Swaminarayan religion|journal=Through Open Doors: A View of Asian Cultures in Kenya|year=1989|page=127}}</ref>

As a result, Yogiji Maharaj made a second visit to East Africa in 1960 and consecrated [[Dev Mandir|hari mandirs]] in Kampala, Jinja and Tororo in Uganda.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|50}} Despite his failing health, Yogiji Maharaj at the age of 78 undertook a third overseas tour of London and East Africa in 1970.<ref name="100y"/>{{rp|169}} Prior to his visit, the devotees had purchased the premises of the Indian Christian Union at [[Ngara, Kenya]] in 1966 and remodeled it to resemble a three-spired temple.<ref name="Salvadori 1989 128">{{cite journal|last=Salvadori|first=Cynthia|title=Revivals, reformations & revisions of Hinduism; the Swaminarayan religion|journal=Through Open Doors: A View of Asian Cultures in Kenya|year=1989|page=128}}</ref> Yogiji Maharaj inaugurated the temple in Ngara, a suburb of Nairobi in 1970.<ref name="Younger 2010 266"/><ref name="Salvadori 1989 128"/>

==== England ====
In 1950, disciples Mahendra Patel and Purushottam Patel held small personal services at their homes in England. Mahendra Patel, a barrister by vocation, writes, "I landed in London in 1950 for further studies. Purushottambhai Patel...was residing in the county of Kent. His address was given to me by Yogiji Maharaj".<ref name="Shelat 2005 17">{{cite book|last=Shelat|first=Kirit|title=Yug Purush Pujya Pramukh Swami Maharaj – a life dedicated to others|year=2005|publisher=Shri Bhagwati Trust|location=Ahmedabad|page=17}}</ref> Beginning 1953, D. D. Meghani held assemblies in his office that brought together several followers in an organized setting. In 1958, leading devotees including Navin Swaminarayan, Praful Patel and Chatranjan Patel from India and East Africa began arriving to the UK.<ref name="Shelat 2005 17"/> They started weekly assemblies at [[Seymour Place]] every Saturday evening at a devotee's house.<ref name="Shelat 2005 17"/> In 1959, a formal constitution was drafted and the group registered as the "Swaminarayan Hindu Mission, London Fellowship Centre".<ref name="Shelat 2005 17"/> D.D. Megani served as chairman, Mahendra Patel as vice-chairman and Praful Patel the secretary.<ref name="Shelat 2005 17"/> On Sunday, 14 June 1970, the first BAPS temple in England was opened at Islington by Yogiji Maharaj.<ref name="Shelat 2005 17"/> In this same year he established the Shree Swaminarayan Mission{{sfn|Dave|1974|p=189}} as a formal organisation.<ref>{{cite book|editor-last=Heath|editor-first=Deana|title=Communalism and globalization in South Asia and its diaspora|year=2010|publisher=Routledge|location=Abingdon, Oxon|isbn=978-0-203-83705-4|page=52|editor2=Mathur, Chandana}}</ref>

==== United States ====
Yogiji Maharaj was unable to travel to the United States during his consecutive foreign tours. Nonetheless, he asked Dr. K.C. Patel to begin satsang assemblies in the United States.<ref name="Kurien 2007 105">{{cite book|last=Kurien|first=Prema A.|title=A place at the multicultural table: the development of an American Hinduism|year=2007|publisher=Rutgers Univ. Press|location=New Brunswick, NJ [u.a.]|isbn=978-0-8135-4056-6|page=105|edition=[Online-Ausg.].}}</ref> He gave Patel the names of 28 Satsangi students to help conduct Satsang assemblies.<ref name="Kurien 2007 105"/>

In 1970, Yogiji Maharaj accepted the request of these students and sent four swami to visit the U.S.<ref name="Kurien 2007 105"/><ref>{{cite book|last=Jones|first=Constance A.|title=Encyclopedia of Hinduism|year=2008|publisher=Checkmark Books|location=New York|isbn=978-0-8160-7336-8|page=431|author2=Ryan, James D.}}</ref> The tour motivated followers to start satsang sabhas in their own homes every Sunday around the country.<ref name="Kurien 2007 105"/> Soon, K.C. Patel established a non-profit organization known as BSS under US law.<ref>{{cite book|last=Kurien|first=Prema A.|title=A place at the multicultural table: the development of an American Hinduism|year=2007|publisher=Rutgers University Press|location=New Brunswick, New Jersey|isbn=978-0-8135-4056-6|page=106|edition=online}}</ref> Thus, a fledgling Satsang mandal formed in the United States before the death of Yogiji Maharaj in 1971.

===Growth and further global expansion (1971–2016)===
[[File:Pramukh_Swami_Maharaj.jpg|alt=|thumb|[[Pramukh Swami Maharaj]]]]
After [[Yogiji Maharaj]]'s death, [[Pramukh Swami Maharaj]] became both the spiritual and administrative head of BAPS{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=77}} in 1971.<ref name=":4">{{Cite book|title=Encyclopedia of religion|last=Jones|first=Lindsay|publisher=Detroit: Macmillan Reference USA/Thomson Gale, c2005.|year=2005|isbn=978-0028659824|pages=8891}}</ref> He was the fifth spiritual guru of the BAPS.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.baps.org/About-BAPS/TheCurrentSpiritualGuru-PramukhSwamiMaharaj/Spiritual-Lineage.aspx|title=Bhagwan Swaminarayan's Spiritual Lineage|website=BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha|access-date=2016-08-29}}</ref> Under his leadership, BAPS has grown into a global Hindu organization and has witnessed expansion in several areas. His work has been built on the foundations laid by his gurus – Shastriji Maharaj and Yogiji Maharaj.

==== Personal outreach (1971–1981) ====
Immediately upon taking helm, Pramukh Swami Maharaj ventured on a hectic spiritual tour in the first decade of his role as the new Spiritual Guru. Despite health conditions—[[cataract]] operation in 1980—he continued to make extensive tours to more than 4000 villages and towns, visiting over 67,000 homes and performing Murti Pratishtha (image installation) ceremonies in 77 temples in this first decade.{{sfn|Williams|2001}} He also embarked on a series of overseas tours beginning in 1974 as the guru. Subsequent tours were made in 1977, 1979, and 1980.<ref>{{Cite book|title=100 Years of BAPS|last=Sadhu|first=Amrutvijaydas|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|date=December 2007|isbn=978-8175263772|location=Amdavad|pages=169}}</ref>

Overall, he embarked on a total of 28 international spiritual tours between 1974 and 2014.<ref name="Kurien 2007 105" /><ref>{{Cite web|title=BAPS Inaugurates Stone Mandir in New Jersey|url=https://www.indiawest.com/news/global_indian/baps-inaugurates-stone-mandir-in-new-jersey/article_79f9da02-2a22-11e4-a37d-001a4bcf887a.html|last=Staff|first=India West|website=India West|language=en|access-date=2020-05-26|archive-date=25 August 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140825222945/http://www.indiawest.com/news/global_indian/baps-inaugurates-stone-mandir-in-new-jersey/article_79f9da02-2a22-11e4-a37d-001a4bcf887a.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> His travels were motivated by his desire to reach out to devotees for their spiritual uplift and to spread the teachings of [[Swaminarayan]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.baps.org/About-BAPS/TheCurrentSpiritualGuru-PramukhSwamiMaharaj/Vicharan.aspx|title=Pramukh Swami's Vicharan|website=BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha|access-date=2016-08-29}}</ref>

==== Festivals and organisation (1981–1992) ====
[[File:BAPS monks.jpg|thumb|Swamis of BAPS with Pramukh Swami Maharaj in Sarangpur, Gujarat, India (2016)]]
The personal outreach (vicharan) of the earlier era (1971–1981) by Pramukh Swami Maharaj through traveling to villages and towns, writing letters to devotees, and giving discourses contributed to sustaining a global BAPS community.

The Gujarati migration patterns in the early 1970s, globalization factors and economic dynamics between India and the West saw the organization transform into a transnational devotional movement.<ref name=":5">{{Cite journal|last=Kim|first=Hanna Hea-Sun|date=2012|title=A Fine Balance: Adaptation and Accommodation in the Swaminarayan Sanstha|journal=Gujarati Communities Across the Globe: Memory, Identity and Continuity. Edited by Sharmina Mawani and Anjoom Mukadam|publisher=Stoke-on-Trent, United Kingdom: Trentham Books|pages=141–156|doi=10.1111/rsr.12043_9}}{{clarify|reason=what is being cited here? the book chapter or the RSR review? cite only one and use the appropriate template;|date=June 2021}}</ref> organizational needs spanned from transmitting cultural identity through spiritual discourses to the newer much alienated generation in the new lands, temple upkeep and traveling to regional and local centres to disseminate spiritual knowledge. As a result, this era saw a significant rise in the number of swamis initiated to maintain the organizational needs of the community – both in India and abroad. Furthermore, having access to a greater volunteer force and community enabled the organization to celebrate festivals on a massive scale which marked the arrival of a number of milestone anniversaries in the history of the organization, including the bicentenary of [[Swaminarayan]], bicentenary of [[Gunatitanand Swami]], and the centenary of [[Yogiji Maharaj]]. Some effects of the celebration included a maturation of organizational capacity, increased commitment and skill of volunteers, and tangentially, an increased interest in the monastic path.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}

The Swaminarayan bicentenary celebration, a once in a life-time event for Swaminarayan followers, was held in [[Ahmedabad]] in April 1981.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p={{page needed|date=June 2021}}}} On 7 March 1981, 207 youths were initiated into the monastic order.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p={{page needed|date=June 2021}}}} In 1985 the bicentenary birth of Gunatitanand Swami was celebrated.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p={{page needed|date=June 2021}}}} During this festival, 200 youths were initiated into the monastic order.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|url=http://www.baps.org/About-BAPS/TheCurrentSpiritualGuru-PramukhSwamiMaharaj/His-Work.aspx|title=Pramukh Swami's Work|website=BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha|access-date=2016-08-29}}</ref>

The organization held Cultural Festivals of India in London in 1985 and New Jersey in 1991.<ref name=":3" /> The month-long Cultural Festival of India was held at Alexandra Palace in London in 1985.<ref name=":3" /> The same festival was shipped to US as a month-long Cultural Festival of India at Middlesex County College in [[Edison, New Jersey]].<ref name="Brear 1996" />

Migrational patterns in the 70s led to a disproportionate number of Hindus in the diaspora.<ref name=":5" /> Culturally, a need arose to celebrate special festivals (Cultural Festival of India) to reach out to youths in the diaspora to foster understanding and appreciation of their mother culture in a context accessible to them.<ref name=":4" /><ref>{{Cite thesis |degree=MA |first=Angela Carol |last=Rudert|date=May 2004 |title=Inherent Faith and Negotiated Power: Swaminarayan Women in the United States |language=en-US |publisher=Cornell University |hdl=1813/106}}</ref>

By the end of the era, owing to the success of these festivals and the cultural impact it had on the youths, the organization saw a need to create a permanent exhibition in the [[Akshardham (Gandhinagar)|Swaminarayan Akshardham (Gandhinagar) temple]] in 1991.

In 1992, a month-long festival was held to both celebrate Yogiji Maharaj's centenary and to inaugurate a permanent exhibition and temple called Swaminarayan Akshardham (Gandhinagar). The festival also saw 125 youths initiated into the monastic order bringing the total number of swamis initiated to more than 700 in fulfillment to a prophecy made by [[Yogiji Maharaj]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.baps.org/News/2003/700-BAPS-Sadhus-1634.aspx|title=700 BAPS Sadhus|website=BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha|access-date=2016-08-29}}</ref>

==== Mandirs and global growth (1992–2016) ====
In the third leg of the era, the organization saw an unprecedented level of mandir construction activities taking place in order to accommodate the rapid rise of adherents across the global Indian diaspora. Initially, beginning with the inauguration of [[Akshardham (Gandhinagar)|Swaminarayan Akshardham (Gandhinagar)]] in 1992. A number of [[shikharbaddha mandir]]s (large traditional stone mandirs) were inaugurated in major cities; [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir London|London]] (1995),<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Bharne |first1=Vinayak |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=CGukBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA248 |title=Rediscovering the Hindu Temple: The Sacred Architecture and Urbanism of India |last2=Krusche |first2=Krupali |publisher=Cambridge Scholars Publishing |date=2014-09-18 |isbn=978-1-4438-6734-4 |pages=248}}</ref> [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Nairobi|Nairobi]] (1999), New Delhi (2004), [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Houston|Houston]] (2004), [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Chicago|Chicago]] (2004), [[Akshardham (Delhi)|Swaminarayan Akshardham (New Delhi)]] (2005),<ref>{{Cite book |last=McCulloch |first=Victoria |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bkH-AgAAQBAJ&dq=delhi+akshardham+opening&pg=PA48 |title=Rajasthan, Delhi & Agra |publisher=Footprint Travel Guides |year=2014 |isbn= 978-1-909268-39-5|pages=48 |language=en}}</ref> [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Toronto|Toronto]] (2007), [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Atlanta|Atlanta]] (2007), [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Chino Hills|Los Angeles]] (2012), and [[Swaminarayan Akshardham (North America)|Robbinsville]] (New Jersey) (2014).<ref>{{Cite web |date=2014-08-23 |title=A Look At The New, Massive Hindu Temple Gracing New Jersey |url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/new-jersey-baps-hindu-temple_n_5668901 |access-date=2024-01-25 |website=HuffPost |language=en}}</ref>

===Mahant Swami Maharaj as Guru (2016 – present)===
[[File:Mahant_Swami_Maharaj.jpg|alt=|thumb|[[Mahant Swami Maharaj]]]]
On 20 July 2012, in the presence of senior swamis in Ahmedabad, Pramukh Swami Maharaj revealed Keshavjivandas Swami (Mahant Swami) as his spiritual successor.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=http://www.baps.org/Announcement/2016/Final-Darshan-and-Rites-of-Pramukh-Swami-Maharaj-9961.aspx|title=Final Darshan and Rites of Pramukh Swami Maharaj|website=BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha|access-date=2016-08-13}}</ref>

Following the death of Pramukh Swami Maharaj on 13 August 2016, Mahant Swami Maharaj became the 6th guru and president of BAPS.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Kulkarni|first=Deepali|date=December 2018|title=Digital Mūrtis, Virtual Darśan and a Hindu Religioscape|url=https://journals.co.za/content/journal/10520/EJC-1273db35e9|journal=Nidān: International Journal for Indian Studies|volume=3|issue=2|pages=40–54}}</ref> In 1961, he was ordained as a swami by Yogiji Maharaj and named Keshavjivandas Swami. Due to his appointment as the head (mahant) of the mandir in Mumbai, he became known as Mahant Swami.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Sacred Architecture and Experience BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir, Robbinsville, New Jersey|last=Mangalnidhidas|first=Sadhu|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|year=2019|isbn=978-19-4746-101-7|location=Ahmedabad, Gujarat, India|pages=100}}</ref>

He continues the legacy of the Aksharbrahma Gurus by visiting BAPS mandirs worldwide, guiding spiritual aspirants, initiating devotees, ordaining swamis, creating and sustaining mandirs, and encouraging the development of scriptures.{{sfn|NN|2019|p=51}}<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.newsindiatimes.com/baps-temple-holds-installation-of-1st-pillar-mandapam-and-visarjan-in-new-jersey/|title=BAPS Temple holds installation of 1st pillar, Mandapam, and Visarjan in New Jersey {{!}} News India Times|last=Writer|first=Staff|date=12 September 2017 |language=en-US|access-date=2019-12-21}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/uae/heritage/everything-we-know-about-the-uae-s-first-traditional-hindu-temple-1.851155|title=Everything we know about the UAE's first traditional Hindu temple|website=The National|date=21 April 2019 |language=en|access-date=2019-12-21}}</ref>

In his discourses, he mainly speaks on how one can attain God and peace through ridding one's ego (nirmani), seeing divinity in all (divyabhav), not seeing, talking, or adapting any negative nature or behavior of others (no abhav-avgun), and keeping unity (samp).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLOX7n0cygMYA19eaokQqliIzEhz--1BoK|title=Guruhari Ashirwad - YouTube|website=YouTube|language=en|access-date=2019-12-21}}</ref>

In 2017, he performed the ground-breaking ceremony for shikharbaddha mandirs in [[Johannesburg|Johannesburg, South Africa]], and [[Sydney, Australia]], and in April 2019, he performed the ground-breaking ceremony for a traditional stone temple in [[BAPS Hindu Mandir Abu Dhabi|Abu Dhabi]].{{sfn|NN|2019|p=51}}

In May 2021, six workers involved in the construction of the [[Swaminarayan Akshardham (North America)|Akshardham temple]] filed suit against the temple administrators resulting in a government investigation of potential labor law violations.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Correal|first=Annie|date=2021-05-11|title=Hindu Sect Is Accused of Using Forced Labor to Build N.J. Temple|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/11/nyregion/nj-hindu-temple-india-baps.html|access-date=2021-05-12|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Spokespersons for BAPS said that the claims were without merit.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Choi|first=Joseph|date=2021-05-11|title=Federal agents probe Hindu sect for using forced labor to build New Jersey temple|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/552899-federal-agents-probe-hindu-sect-for-using-forced-labor-to-build-new|access-date=2021-05-12|website=TheHill|language=en}}</ref> In November 2021, the workers amended the suit to include BAPS temples across the United States and alleged that the temple authorities had misrepresented unskilled workers as specialists in stone carving and painting so that they qualified for [[R visa|R-1 visas]]. The lawsuit claims that the workers are recruited from [[Dalit]]s and [[Adivasi]]s, both marginalized communities in India.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Correal|first=Annie|date=10 November 2021|title=Hindu Sect Accused of Using Forced Labor at More Temples Across U.S.|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/11/10/nyregion/baps-hindu-forced-labor.html}}</ref>

In July 2023, a dozen plaintiffs voluntarily withdrew their names from the lawsuit, citing that they were coerced and misled by an immigration advocate, Swati Sawant, into making false accusations against BAPS to stall the construction of the temple. The workers stated that Sawant lured them with a promise of citizenship in the US and gave false warnings of police action and imprisonment if they came forward and told the truth about the false allegations.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2023-07-16 |title='Charges against Robbinsville Akshardham temple false' |work=The Times of India |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/ahmedabad/charges-against-robbinsville-akshardham-temple-false/articleshow/101793020.cms |access-date=2023-07-24 |issn=0971-8257}}</ref>

==Akshar-Purshottam Upasana==
[[File:Swaminarayan Bhashyam.jpg|alt=|thumb|Swaminarayan Bhashyam]]
{{Main|Akshar-Purushottam Upasana}}

===Akshar===
The philosophy of BAPS is centred on the doctrine of [[Akshar-Purushottam Upasana]], in which followers worship [[Swaminarayan]] as God, or Purshottam, and his choicest devotee, [[Gunatitanand Swami]], as Akshar.{{sfn|Williams|1984|p=73}} The BAPS concurs that Akshar is the divine abode of Purushottam and "an eternally existing spiritual reality having two forms, the impersonal and the personal."{{sfn|Williams|1984|p=73}}<ref>Gadhada I-21 The Vachanamrut: Spiritual Discourses of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. Ahmedabad: Swaminarayan Aksharpith. 2003. 2nd edition. {{ISBN|81-7526-190-0}}, pg. 31.</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite book|title=An Introduction to Swaminarayan Hindu Theology|last=Paramtattvadas|first=Swami|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year=2017|isbn=9781316611272}}</ref>{{rp|158}} Followers of BAPS identify various scriptures and documented statements of Swaminarayan as supporting this understanding of Akshar within the Akshar-Purushottam Upasana.<ref name="Williams2004" />{{rp|95–103}} Through this lineage of the personal form of Akshar, Swaminarayan is forever present on the earth.{{sfn|Williams|2001|pp=55, 92}} These gurus are essential in illuminating the path that needs to be taken by the jivas that earnestly desire to be liberated from the cycle of rebirth.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=65}}{{refn|group=note|Members of BAPS point to numerous historical anecdotes and scriptural references, particularly from the central Swaminarayan text known as the [[Vachanamrut]], as veritable evidence that Gunatitanand Swami was the manifest form of Akshar.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=76}} Swaminarayan refers to this concept specifically in Vachnamrut Gadhada I-21, Gadhada I-71, Gadhada III-26, Vadtal 5.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=92}} Following Gunatitanand Swami, the lineage continued on through Bhagatji Maharaj (1829–1897), Shastriji Maharaj (1865–1951), Yogiji Maharaj (1892–1971), and Pramukh Swami Maharaj (1921–2016). Today Mahant Swami Maharaj is said to be the manifest form of Akshar.<ref name=":2" />}}

According to BAPS, Swaminarayan refers to Akshar in the Vachanamrut, with numerous appellations such as Sant, Satpurush, Bhakta and Swami, as having an august status that makes it an entity worth worshipping alongside God.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=453–455}}{{refn|group=note|For example, in Vachanamrut Gadhada I-37, Swaminarayan states: "In fact, the darshan of such a true Bhakta of God is equivalent to the darshan of God Himself"<ref>Vachanamrut, Gadhada I-37, The Vachanamrut: Spiritual Discourses of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. Ahmedabad: Swaminarayan Aksharpith. 2003. 2nd edition. {{ISBN|81-7526-190-0}}, p. 65</ref> Moreover, in Vachanamrut Vadtal 5, Swaminarayan states: "Just as one performs the mãnsi puja of God, if one also performs the mãnsi puja of the ideal Bhakta along with God, by offering him the prasãd of God; and just as one prepares a thãl for God, similarly, if one also prepares a thãl for God's ideal Bhakta and serves it to him; and just as one donates five rupees to God, similarly, if one also donates money to the great Sant – then by performing with extreme affection such similar service of God and the Sant who possesses the highest qualities...he will become a devotee of the highest calibre in this very life."<ref>Vartal 5. The Vachanamrut: Spiritual Discourses of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. Ahmedabad: Swaminarayan Aksharpith. 2003. 2nd edition. p. 536–537. {{ISBN|81-7526-190-0}}</ref>}} In all BAPS mandirs the image of Akshar is placed in the central shrine and worshipped alongside the image of Purushottam.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=86}}{{sfn|Williams|1984|p=112}} Furthermore, BAPS believes that by understanding the greatness of God's choicest devotee, coupled with devotion and service to him and God, followers are able to grow spiritually.{{refn|group=note|This practice is mentioned by Swaminarayan in Vachanamrut Vadtal 5: "by performing with extreme affection such similar service of God and the Sant who possesses the highest qualities, even if he is a devotee of the lowest type and was destined to become a devotee of the highest type after two lives, or after four lives, or after ten lives, or after 100 lives, he will become a devotee of the highest caliber in this very life. Such are the fruits of the similar service of God and God's Bhakta."<ref>Vachanamrut, Vartal 5, The Vachanamrut: Spiritual Discourses of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. Ahmedabad: Swaminarayan Aksharpith. 2003. 2nd edition, p.534</ref>}}

===Moksha===
According to BAPS doctrines, followers aim to attain a spiritual state similar to Brahman which is ultimate liberation.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=291}} To become an ideal Hindu, followers must identify with Brahman, separate from the material body, and offer devotion to god.<ref>Shikshapatri, shloka 116.</ref><ref name=":6" />{{rp|276}} As per the Akshar-Purushottam Upasana, each jiva attains liberation and true realization through association with the manifest form of Akshar, in the form of the God-realized guru, who offer spiritual guidance.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=295, 325}}<ref>G. Sundaram. The Concept of Social Service in the Philosophy of Sri Swaminarayan. p. 45</ref> Jivas who perform devotion to this personal form of Brahman can, despite remaining ontologically different, attain a similar spiritual standing as Brahman and then go to Akshardham.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=319–320}}<ref>Mukundcharandas, Sadhu (2004). Vachnamrut Handbook (Insights into Bhagwan Swaminarayan's Teachings). Amdavad: Swaminarayan Aksharpith. {{ISBN|81-7526-263-X}} p. 160</ref> It is only through the performance of devotion to Brahman that [[Para Brahman|Parabrahman]] can be both realized and attained.<ref>G. Sundaram. The Concept of Social Service in the Philosophy of Sri Swaminarayan. p. 43</ref>

===Ekantik dharma===
Devotees aim to follow the spiritual guidance of the manifest form of Akshar embedding the principles of righteousness (dharma), knowledge (gnan), detachment from material pleasures (vairagya) and devotion unto God (bhakti) into their lives.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=358}}

Followers receive gnan through regularly listening to spiritual discourses and reading scriptures in an effort to gain knowledge of God and one's true self.<ref name="swaminarayan.org">{{Cite web | url=http://www.swaminarayan.org/faq/bapsgeneral.htm |title = FAQ-Related to BAPS Swaminaryan Sansthã-General}}</ref>

Dharma encompasses righteous conduct as prescribed by the scriptures.<ref name="swaminarayan.org"/> The ideals of dharma range from devotees following the principle of [[ahimsa]] (non-violence), practicing [[Lacto vegetarianism|lacto-vegetarianism]],<ref>Clarke, Matthew. (2011). ''Development and Religion: Theology and Practice''. Edward Elgar. p. 42. {{ISBN|978-0857930736}}</ref> avoiding onions, garlic, and other items in their diet.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vegetarianism |url=https://www.baps.org/Spiritual-Living/Hindu-Beliefs/Compassion-and-Nonviolence-Ahimsa/Vegetarianism.aspx |access-date=2024-02-23 |website=BAPS |language=en-US}}</ref> [[Swaminarayan]] outlined the dharma of his devotees in the scripture the Shikshapatri.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=456}}<ref name=":7">{{Cite book|title=Global Religious Movements Across Borders: Sacred Service|last=Brahmbhatt|first=Arun|publisher=Ashgate Publishing|year=2014|isbn=978-1-409-45687-2|location=Oxon}}</ref>{{rp|Ch 5 - Pg 2}} He included practical aspects of living life such as not committing adultery and respecting elders, gurus, and those of authority.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=333}}

Devotees develop detachment ([[vairāgya|vairagya]]) in order to spiritually elevate their soul ([[jiva]]) to a Brahmic state. This entails practices such as biweekly fasting (on the eleventh day of each half of each lunar month) and avoiding worldly pleasures by strongly attaching themselves to God.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=326}}

The fourth pillar, devotion (bhakti) is at the heart of the faith community. Common practices of devotion include daily prayers, offering prepared dishes (thal) to the image of God, mental worship of God and his ideal devotee, and singing religious hymns.<ref name="swaminarayan.org"/> Spiritual service, or seva, is a form of devotion where devotees serve selflessly "while keeping only the Lord in mind."{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=343}}

Followers participate in various socio-spiritual activities with the objective to earn the grace of the guru and thus attain association with God through voluntary service.<ref name="Williams2004">Williams, Raymond Brady (2004). "The Holy Man as the Abode of God in Swaminarayan Hinduism". Williams on South Asian Religions and Immigration; Collected Works (Ashgate Publishing, Ltd.)</ref>{{rp|97}} These numerous activities stem directly from the ideals taught by Swaminarayan, to find spiritual devotion in the service of others.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=319–320, 389}} By serving and volunteering in communities to please the guru, devotees are considered to be serving the guru.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=389}} This relationship is the driving force for the spiritual actions of devotees. The guru is [[Mahant Swami Maharaj]], who is held to be the embodiment of selfless devotion. Under the guidance of Mahant Swami Maharaj, followers observe the tenets of Swaminarayan through the above-mentioned practices, striving to please the guru and become close to God.<ref name=":2" />

==Temples==
The [[mandir]], known as a [[Hindu]] place of worship, serves as a hub for the spiritual, cultural, and humanitarian activities of BAPS. As of 2024, the organisation has 44 shikharbaddha mandirs and more than 1,300 other mandirs spanning five continents.{{sfn|NN|2019|p=51}} In the tradition of the [[Bhakti Movement]], [[Swaminarayan]] and his spiritual successors began erecting mandirs to provide a means to uphold proper devotion to God on the path towards [[moksha]], or ultimate liberation.<ref name="Gadhada II-27">{{cite book|last=Gadhada II-27|title=The Vachanamrut: Spiritual Discourses of Bhagwan Swaminarayan|year=2001|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|location=Amdavad|isbn=81-7526-190-0}}</ref>{{rp|440}} BAPS mandirs thus facilitate devotional commitment to the [[Akshar-Purushottam Darshan]], in which followers strive to reach the spiritually perfect state of Aksharbrahman, or the ideal devotee, thereby gaining the ability to properly worship Purushottam, the Supreme Godhead.{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=7–13}}

===Temple rituals===
The offering of [[bhakti]], or devotion to God, remains at the centre of mandir activities. In all BAPS Swaminarayan mandirs, [[murtis]], or sacred images of [[Swaminarayan]], [[Gunatitanand Swami]], BAPS gurus and other deities, are enshrined in the [[Garbhagriha|inner sanctum]]. After completion of [[prana pratishta]] or life-force installation ceremonies, the deities are believed to reside in the murtis and are thus subjects of direct worship through sacred daily rituals.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=240}} In many mandirs, murtis are adorned with clothes and ornaments and devotees come to perform [[Darśana|darshan]], the act of worshiping the deity by viewing the sacred image.{{sfn|Williams|2001|pp=131, 140}}<ref name="EoH 119">{{cite book|last=Jones|first=Constance|title=Encyclopedia of Hinduism|year=2007|publisher=Infobase Publishing|location=New York|isbn=978-0-8160-7336-8|pages=119}}</ref> [[Aarti]], which is a ritual of waving lit lamps in circular motions to illuminate the different parts of the murti while singing a song of praise, is performed five times daily in [[Shikharband Mandir|shikharbaddha mandirs]] and twice daily in smaller [[Dev Mandir|mandirs]]. Additionally, food is offered to the murtis amidst the singing of devotional songs three times a day as part of the ritual of [[thaal]], and the sanctified food is then distributed to devotees.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=140}}  Daily readings of and discourses on various Hindu scriptures also take place in the mandir.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=132}}  Many mandirs are also home to BAPS swamis, or monks.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=50}}  On weekends, assemblies are held in which swamis and devotees deliver discourses on a variety of spiritual topics. During these assemblies, bhakti is offered in the form of call-and-response hymns (''[[kirtans]]'') with traditional musical accompaniment. Religious assemblies also take place for children and teenagers of various age ranges.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=62}}  Throughout the year, mandirs celebrate traditional Hindu festivals. Assemblies with special discourses, kirtans, and other performances are arranged to commemorate [[Swaminarayan Jayanti|Rama Navami]], [[Janmashtami]], [[Diwali]], and other major Hindu holidays.{{sfn|Williams|2001|pp=138–147}} Members of the sect are known as [[Satsangi]]s. Male Satsangis are generally initiated by obtaining a [[Kanthi Mala|kanthi]] at the hands of a swamis or senior male devotee while females receive the vartman from the senior women followers.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Hindu rites & rituals: sentiments, sacraments & symbols|last=Mukundcharandas, Sadhu.|date=2007|publisher=Swaminarayan Aksharpith|others=Das, Jnaneshwar., Swaminarayan Aksharpith.|isbn=978-8175263567|edition=1st|location=Amdavad, India|oclc=464166168}}</ref>{{Rp|273–276}}

===Temple activities===
[[File:Mahant_Swami_Maharaj_performs_the_arti.jpg|alt=|thumb|248x248px|[[Mahant Swami Maharaj]] performing the arti]]
In addition to being focal points of religious activity, BAPS mandirs are also centres of culture.{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=21}} Many forms of traditional Indian art have their roots in Hindu scriptures and have been preserved and flourished in the setting of mandirs.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=220}} Many BAPS mandirs outside of India hold [[Gujarati language|Gujarati]] classes to facilitate scriptural study, instruction in traditional dance forms in preparation for performances in festival assemblies, and music classes where students are taught how to play traditional instruments such as [[tabla]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Shri Swaminarayan Mandir|url=http://pluralism.org/profiles/view/71207|work=Pluralism Project|publisher=Harvard University|access-date=11 January 2013|archive-date=20 May 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130520221525/http://pluralism.org/profiles/view/71207|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Indiapost Harijayanti">{{cite news|title=BAPS celebrates Swaminarayan Jayanti & Ram Navmi|url=http://www.indiapost.com/baps-celebrates-swaminarayan-jayanti-ram-navmi/|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=India Post|date=22 April 2011}}</ref> Devotees view the mandir as a place for transmission of knowledge of Hindu values and their incorporation into daily routines, family life, and careers.{{sfn|Kim|2001|pp=418–422}}<ref group=web name="India Herald Spiritual Quotient"/>

Apart from classes teaching about religion and culture, mandirs are also the site of activities focused on youth development. Many centres organize college preparatory classes, leadership training seminars and workplace skills development workshops.<ref name="Atlanta Conf">{{cite news|last=Dave|first=Hiral|title=Baps tells Youth to uphold values|url=http://www.dnaindia.com/world/report_baps-tells-youth-to-uphold-values_1232640|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=Daily News & Analysis|date=20 February 2009}}</ref><ref name="Kalam Youth">{{cite news|title=Strive to be unique, Kalam tells youth|url=http://www.indiapost.com/strive-to-be-unique-kalam-tells-youth/|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=India Post|date=29 April 2011}}</ref><ref name="Chino Hills Youth">{{cite web|last=Patel|first=Divyesh|title=BAPS Commemorates Education with Educational Development Day|url=http://www.chinohills.com/news-articles-details/BAPS_Commemorates_Education_with_Educational_Development_Day-1542|work=ChinoHills.com|access-date=11 January 2013|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130222020011/http://www.chinohills.com/news-articles-details/BAPS_Commemorates_Education_with_Educational_Development_Day-1542|archive-date=22 February 2013}}</ref> Centres often host women's conferences aimed at empowering women.<ref name="Chicago Women's">{{cite news|last=Padmanabhan|first=R|title=BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha Hosts Women's Conference in Chicago|url=http://www.nritoday.net/women/287-aug-08-baps-swaminarayan-sanstha-hosts-womens-conference-in-chicago|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=NRI Today|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130916133018/http://www.nritoday.net/women/287-aug-08-baps-swaminarayan-sanstha-hosts-womens-conference-in-chicago|archive-date=16 September 2013}}</ref> They also host sports tournaments and initiatives to promote healthy lifestyles among children and youth.<ref name="Childrens Health">{{cite news|last=Piccirilli|first=Amanda|title=BAPS Charities stresses importance of children's health|url=http://www.timesherald.com/article/20120813/NEWS03/120819840/baps-charities-stresses-importance-of-children-146-s-health|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=The Times Herald|date=13 August 2012|archive-date=22 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140522045756/http://www.timesherald.com/article/20120813/NEWS03/120819840/baps-charities-stresses-importance-of-children-146-s-health|url-status=dead}}</ref> Many centres also host parenting seminars, marriage counseling, and events for family bonding.<ref name="Hinduism Today Parenting">{{cite web|title=Parents Are the Key|url=http://www.hinduismtoday.com/modules/smartsection/print.php?itemid=1432|publisher=Hinduism Today|access-date=11 January 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=BAPS Charities|url=http://berkleycenter.georgetown.edu/resources/organizations/baps-charities|publisher=Georgetown University|access-date=11 January 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140310105217/http://berkleycenter.georgetown.edu/resources/organizations/baps-charities|archive-date=10 March 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref>

BAPS mandirs and cultural centres serve as hubs of several humanitarian activities powered by local volunteers. Mandirs in the US and UK host an annual walkathon to raise funds for local charities such as hospitals or schools.<ref>{{cite news|last=Rajda|first=K|title=Local communities join BAPS Walkathon|url=http://www.indiapost.com/local-communities-join-baps-walkathon|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=IndiaPost|date=29 June 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=BAPS Charities Hosts 15th Annual Walkathon|url=http://www.indiawest.com/news/4867-baps-charities-hosts-15th-annual-walkathon.html|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=India West|date=6 June 2012|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120608222818/http://www.indiawest.com/news/4867-baps-charities-hosts-15th-annual-walkathon.html|archive-date=8 June 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=BAPS Charities Host Community Walk in Support of American Diabetes Association and Stafford MSD Education Fund|url=http://www.indoamerican-news.com/archives/7136|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=Indo American News}}{{dead link|date=July 2017 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Centres also host annual health fairs where needy members of the community can undergo health screenings and consultations.<ref>{{cite web|last=Gibson|first=Michael|title=BAPS Charities Health Fair 2012|date=10 August 2012 |url=http://www.nbcdfw.com/on-air/community/BAPS-Charities-Health-Fair-2012-165775856.html|publisher=NBCUniversal|access-date=11 January 2013}}</ref> During weekend assemblies, physicians are periodically invited to speak on various aspects of preventative medicine and to raise awareness on common conditions.<ref>{{cite web|last=Patel|first=Divyesh|title=BAPS Charities to Hold Women's FREE Health Awareness Lectures 2010 on Saturday, June 5th 2010|url=http://www.chinohills.com/news-articles-details/BAPS_Charities_to_Hold_Womens_FREE_Health_Awareness_Lectures_2010_on_Saturday_June_5th_2010_-1343|work=ChinoHills.com|access-date=11 January 2013|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130728033711/http://www.chinohills.com/news-articles-details/BAPS_Charities_to_Hold_Womens_FREE_Health_Awareness_Lectures_2010_on_Saturday_June_5th_2010_-1343|archive-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> In times of disaster, centres closest to the affected area become hubs for relief activity ranging from providing meals to reconstructing communities.<ref>{{cite news|title=BAPS Care International launches relief efforts for Hurricane Katrina victims|url=http://india-herald.com/baps-care-international-launches-relief-efforts-for-hurricane-katrina-victi-p801-1.htm|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=India Herald}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=BAPS Charities helps victims of tornadoes|url=http://www.indiapost.com/baps-charities-helps-victims-of-tornadoes/|access-date=11 January 2013|newspaper=IndiaPost|date=20 May 2011}}</ref>
