The founder of BAPS, [[Shastriji Maharaj]], built the first mandir in [[Bochasan]], Gujarat, which led the organisation to be known as "Bochasanwasi" (of Bochasan).{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=8–9}}

The organisation's second mandir was built in [[Salangpur|Sarangpur]], which also hosts a seminary for BAPS swamis.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=112}}

The mandir in [[Gondal, India|Gondal]] was constructed around the [[Akshar Deri]], the cremation memorial of [[Gunatitanand Swami]], who is revered as a manifestation of Aksharbrahman.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=132}}

Shastriji Maharaj constructed his last mandir on the banks of the [[River Ghela]] in [[Gadhada]], where [[Swaminarayan]] resided for the majority of his adult life.{{sfn|Williams|2001|p=19}}{{sfn|Kim|2009|p=9}}

[[Yogiji Maharaj]] constructed the mandir in the Shahibaug section of [[Ahmedabad]], which remains the site of the international headquarters of the organisation.{{sfn|Kim|2001|p=86}}

Under the leadership of [[Pramukh Swami Maharaj]], over 25 additional shikharbaddha mandirs have been built across [[Gujarat]] and other regions of India and abroad.

As a consequence of the Indian emigration patterns, mandirs have been constructed in Africa, Europe, North America, and the Asia-Pacific region.{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=13–14}}  The [[BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir London|BAPS mandir in Neasden]], London was the first traditional Hindu mandir built in Europe.{{sfn|Kim|2009|pp=11–12}} The organisation has six shikharbaddha mandir's in North America in the metro areas of Houston, Chicago, Atlanta, Toronto, Los Angeles, and in the [[New Jersey]] suburb of [[Robbinsville Township, New Jersey|Robbinsville Township]], near Trenton, New Jersey.<ref>{{Cite web | url=http://www.nj.com/mercer/index.ssf/2014/07/traditional_hindu_temple_finds_home_in_robbinsville_just_off_route_130.html |title = Traditional Hindu temple opening in Robbinsville, part of BAPS community|date = 29 July 2014}}</ref>

BAPS has constructed three large temple complexes dedicated to Swaminarayan called [[Akshardham (religion)|Swaminarayan Akshardham]] in [[Akshardham (Delhi)|New Delhi]], [[Akshardham (Gandhinagar)|Gandhinagar]], Gujarat, and [[Swaminarayan Akshardham (New Jersey)|Robbinsville]], New Jersey,<ref>{{Cite web |title=PM Modi, Rishi Sunak extend wishes for Akshardham temple inauguration in USA |url=https://www.indiatoday.in/world/story/pm-modi-rishi-sunak-extend-wishes-for-akshardham-temple-inauguration-in-us-2443555-2023-10-03 |access-date=2024-01-27 |website=India Today |date=3 October 2023 |language=en}}</ref> which in addition to a large stone-carved mandir has exhibitions that explain Hindu traditions and Swaminarayan history and values.<ref>{{cite book|last=Kim|first=Hanna|title=Gujaratis in the West: Evolving Identities in Contemporary Society, Ch. 4: Edifice Complex: Swaminarayan Bodies and Buildings in the Diaspora |year=2007|publisher=Cambridge Scholars Publishing|location=Newcastle, UK|isbn=978-1-84718-368-2|pages=67–70}}</ref>

[[BAPS Hindu Mandir Abu Dhabi]] is a temple in the Middle East, in the capital city of [[United Arab Emirates]], on 55,000 square metres of land and open to people of all religious backgrounds. The [[Indian Prime Minister]] [[Narendra Modi]] participated in the foundation stone-laying ceremony (2018) in the UAE, which is home to over three million people of Indian origin.<ref name="Correal">{{cite web | last=Correal | first=Annie | title=Hindu Sect Known as BAPS is Accused of Using Forced Labor to Build New Jersey Temple | website=The New York Times | date=2021-05-11 | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/11/nyregion/nj-hindu-temple-india-baps.html | access-date=2024-11-04}}</ref><ref name="Werleman"/><ref name="aljazeera.com">{{cite web | title=Suit accusing Hindu group of forced labour widens to 5 US states | website=Al Jazeera | date=2021-11-11 | url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/11/11/human-trafficking-suit-spreads-to-hindu-temples-in-five-us-states | access-date=2024-12-10}}</ref><ref name="clarionindia.net">{{cite web | title=After Raiding New Jersey Temple Run by BJP-Linked Group, US Agencies Begin Probe | website=Clarion India | date=2021-05-14 | url=https://clarionindia.net/after-raiding-new-jersey-temple-run-by-bjp-linked-group-us-agencies-begin-probe/ | access-date=2024-12-10}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/pm-modi-officially-launches-foundation-stone-laying-ceremony-for-first-hindu-temple-in-abu-dhabi/articleshow/62870719.cms|title=PM Modi officially launches foundation stone-laying ceremony for first Hindu temple in Abu Dhabi – Times of India ►|work=The Times of India|access-date=2018-02-11}}</ref> It was inaugurated on 14 January 2024 by the Indian Prime Minister.<ref>{{cite news |title=PM Modi to inaugurate BAPS temple in UAE: Its special features, architecture, significance |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/explained/explained-global/pm-modi-inaugurate-baps-temple-in-uae-features-9159741/ |access-date=17 February 2024 |work=The Indian Express |date=13 February 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

== BAPS Charities ==
{{main|BAPS Charities}}[[BAPS Charities]] is a religious charitable organization that originates from the Bochasanwasi Akshar Purushottam Swaminarayan Sanstha.{{sfn|Clarke|2011|pp=40–49}} Their history of service is inspired by [[Swaminarayan|Swaminarayan (1781-1830)]], who opened alms houses, built shelters, worked against addiction, and abolished the practice of [[Sati (practice)|sati]] and female infanticide with the goals of removing suffering and effecting positive social change.{{sfnm|Clarke|2011|1pp=40–49|Paranjape|2005|2p=119}} This focus on service to society is stated in the organisation's vision, that "every individual deserves the right to a peaceful, dignified, and healthy way of life. And by improving the quality of life of the individual, we are bettering families, communities, our world, and our future."{{sfn|Clarke|2011|pp=40–49}}

==Controversies==
===Allegations of sexual harassment===
In 2013, the BAPS faced controversy following allegations of [[sexual harassment]] by Swami Priyadarshandas, a former [[sadhu]] of BAPS. Initiated into BAPS at age 14 on December 3, 1973, he filed a 17-page handwritten complaint with the [[Ahmedabad City Police|Ahmedabad city police]] on October 23, 2013. He alleged that Pramukh Swami Maharaj and other sadhus [[Sexual abuse|sexually abused]] him, detailing specific dates and locations, including [[Sarangpur, Gujarat|Sarangpur]]. The case was forwarded to Ahmedabad rural police but saw no documented investigation. BAPS rejected the allegations as baseless, asserting Pramukh Swami’s [[celibacy]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=BAPS refutes allegations by former sadhus |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/ahmedabad/baps-refutes-allegations-by-former-sadhus/ |work=The Indian Express}}</ref><ref>BAPS, [https://www.baps.org/Announcement/2013/Message-for-All-5347.aspx Message for all]</ref>

===Allegations of forced labour and human trafficking===
In May 2021, a lawsuit was filed against BAPS by several volunteer artisans from India<ref name="Washington Post">{{Cite news |date=2023-09-29 |title=The largest Hindu temple on American soil will soon open its doors |language=en-US |newspaper=Washington Post |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/religion/2023/09/29/hindu-temple-robbinsville-nj/ |access-date=2023-11-03 |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> who were involved in the construction of [[Swaminarayan Akshardham (Robbinsville, New Jersey)|Swaminarayan Akshardham]] in [[Robbinsville Township, New Jersey|Robbinsville]], alleging that the temple administrators violated [[United States labor law|labor laws]].<ref name="nytimesA" /> In relation to this, the [[Federal Bureau of Investigation]] (FBI), [[United States Department of Labor|Department of Labor]] (DOL), and [[United States Department of Homeland Security|Department of Homeland Security]] (DHS) visited the site on "court-authorized law enforcement activity."<ref name="nytimesA">{{Cite news |last=Correal |first=Annie |date=2021-05-11 |title=Hindu Sect Is Accused of Using Forced Labor to Build N.J. Temple |language=en-US |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/11/nyregion/nj-hindu-temple-india-baps.html |access-date=2021-05-12 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=2021-05-12 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210512002709/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/11/nyregion/nj-hindu-temple-india-baps.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="CNNA">{{cite news |last1=Simko-Bednarski |first1=Evan |title=Lawsuit claims New Jersey Hindu temple was built on forced labor |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/05/12/us/new-jersey-baps-hindu-temple-suit/index.html |access-date=15 May 2021 |work=[[CNN]]|date=11 May 2021 |archive-date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513131944/https://www.cnn.com/2021/05/12/us/new-jersey-baps-hindu-temple-suit/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The lawsuit alleges that over 200 Indian men, mostly of the [[Dalit]] caste, were brought from India to the US and were subject to [[forced labor]], [[human trafficking]] and were [[wage theft|paid $1 an hour for their work]].<ref name="Correal"/><ref name="NBC-labor">{{Cite news |last=Silva |first=Daniella |date=May 11, 2021 |title=Hindu temple in New Jersey accused of 'shocking violations' in forced-labor lawsuit |work=[[NBC News]] |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/hindu-temple-new-jersey-accused-shocking-violations-forced-labor-lawsuit-n1267041 |access-date=May 27, 2021 |archive-date=May 28, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210528184143/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/hindu-temple-new-jersey-accused-shocking-violations-forced-labor-lawsuit-n1267041 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="nytimesA" />

As of July 2023, 12 of the plaintiffs have withdrawn from the lawsuit. Aaditya Soni, their lawyer, stated that the plaintiffs believed the facts of the case were false and cited religious convictions as the basis for their withdrawal.<ref name=":14">{{Cite news |last=Bailey |first=Sarah Pulliam |date=2023-10-21 |title=A $96 Million Hindu Temple Opens Amid Accusations of Forced Labor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/10/21/nyregion/nj-hindu-temple.html |access-date=2024-01-30 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The lawsuit is on-hold, pending an investigation.<ref name="Washington Post"/>

==Reception==
===Political affiliation===
The BAPS has a "close relationship" with Indian prime minister [[Narendra Modi]] and his ruling [[Bharatiya Janata Party]], and organisations like the [[Hindu American Foundation]].<ref name="Correal"/><ref name="Werleman">{{cite web | last=Werleman | first=CJ | title=Werleman's Worldview: The Groups Promoting Hindutva and Human Slavery in the US | website=Byline Times | date=2022-05-06 | url=https://bylinetimes.com/2022/05/06/werlemans-worldview-the-groups-promoting-hindutva-and-human-slavery-in-the-us/ | access-date=2024-12-10}}</ref><ref name="aljazeera.com"/><ref name="clarionindia.net"/> CJ Werleman cites these ties with the ruling party as the basis for Indian right-wing's defence of BAPS in the [[Swaminarayan Akshardham (Robbinsville, New Jersey)#Lawsuit|Akshardham temple lawsuit controversy]]<ref name="Werleman"/> The right-wing media in India has faced criticism for spreading false narratives that blamed the contractor for the violations of labor norms, wage theft and human trafficking in an effort to defend BAPS.<ref>{{cite web | title=Right Wing Pushes False Narrative to Defend Hindu Sect Accused of Labour Violations in US | website=The Wire | date=2021-05-14 | url=https://thewire.in/religion/baps-labour-violations-us-contractor-false-narrative-right-wing | access-date=2024-12-12}}</ref>

Martha Nussbaum has noted that there exists "a perception of a close link between the Swaminarayan sect and right-wing Gujarati politics." She further asserts that BJP politicians such as L.K. Advani and Narendra Modi have appeared as prominent guests at its events and that members of the organisation have played a significant role in fundraising for the BJP, which has raised concerns among those who wished to put an end to the communal tensions. While the public perception connecting Pramukh Swami to communal tensions has a " flimsy basis", the sect is "widely suspected of having some connection with the violence in Gujarat".<ref name="Nussbaum">{{cite book | last=Nussbaum | first=Martha C. | title=The Clash Within: Democracy, Religious Violence, and India's Future | publisher=Harvard University Press | year=2007 | isbn=978-0-674-02482-3 | jstor=j.ctt13x0k4v | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt13x0k4v | access-date=2024-12-11 | pages=324–326 | doi=10.2307/j.ctt13x0k4v }}</ref>

The sadhus from the sect have denied adherence to any political views and statements from Pramukh Swami assert that their relationship with political figures is based on shared religious identity rather than political collaboration. Nussbaum also notes the complexity of Gujarati civil society and difficulties in assessing the validity of the said public perception. She concludes by stating that the sect "is a rather passive force for peace" and attributes the communal values among the followers of the sect and the BJP to the communal division present in Gujarati civil society, rather than to any malign teachings by the sect.<ref name="Nussbaum"/>

===Practices===
Nussbaum also expresses concerns regarding the sect's practice of "isolating (and implicitly denigrating) women" and absolute veneration to the Pramukh Swami's words, which according to her "reinforces the devaluation of critical and independent thinking that is all too prominent in Gujarat."<ref name="Nussbaum"/>

